{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1579251722,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI5NTczOTA3MTM="},"added":["severity-normal"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1579251722,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI5NTczOTA3MTQ="},"added":["UX"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1579303482,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU3NTgzMjk2MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/4038#issuecomment-575832960"},"message":"Thanks for writing up the details.  Just yesterday I began looking into `GitRepo.fetch` dropping some information.  I wonder if this will end up being the same source.\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003edropped fetch output\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nWhen fetching with the default `--recurse-submodules` behavior, `git fetch` and `repo.git.fetch` clearly report the \"Fetching submodule sub\" output, which makes it clear that the failure below pertains to it.  `GitRepo.fetch` drops that line.\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\n\nset -eu\n\nquiet_git () {\n    git \"$@\" \u003e/dev/null 2\u003e\u00261\n}\n\nrun () {\n    (\n        cd \"$(mktemp -d --tmpdir dl-XXXXXXX)\"\n        quiet_git init a\n        quiet_git init a/sub\n        quiet_git -C a/sub commit --allow-empty -mc1\n        quiet_git -C a submodule add ./sub sub\n        quiet_git -C a commit -m\"add sub\"\n\n        quiet_git clone --recurse-submodules -o rem a b\n        quiet_git -C b/sub remote rename origin rem\n\n        quiet_git -C a/sub commit --allow-empty -mc2\n        quiet_git -C a commit -a -m\"change sub\"\n        echo \"# $@\"\n        \"$@\" || true\n        echo\n        echo\n    )\n}\n\nrun git -C b fetch\nrun python -c \"from datalad.support.gitrepo import GitRepo; GitRepo('b').fetch()\"\nrun python -c \"from datalad.support.gitrepo import GitRepo; GitRepo('b').repo.git.fetch()\"\n```\n\n```\n# git -C b fetch\nFrom /tmp/dl-jdxpgG8/a\n   7e402ad..ef618aa  master     -\u003e rem/master\nFetching submodule sub\nfatal: 'origin' does not appear to be a git repository\nfatal: Could not read from remote repository.\n\nPlease make sure you have the correct access rights\nand the repository exists.\n\n\n# python -c from datalad.support.gitrepo import GitRepo; GitRepo('b').fetch()\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"\u003cstring\u003e\", line 1, in \u003cmodule\u003e\n  File \"/home/kyle/src/python/datalad/datalad/support/gitrepo.py\", line 412, in wrapped\n    return func(repo, *args, **kwargs)\n  File \"/home/kyle/src/python/datalad/datalad/support/gitrepo.py\", line 2174, in fetch\n    **kwargs\n  File \"/home/kyle/src/python/datalad/datalad/support/gitrepo.py\", line 2203, in _call_gitpy_with_progress\n    ret = callable(**git_kwargs)\n  File \"/home/kyle/src/python/gitpython/git/remote.py\", line 792, in fetch\n    res = self._get_fetch_info_from_stderr(proc, progress)\n  File \"/home/kyle/src/python/gitpython/git/remote.py\", line 676, in _get_fetch_info_from_stderr\n    proc.wait(stderr=stderr_text)\n  File \"/home/kyle/src/python/gitpython/git/cmd.py\", line 417, in wait\n    raise GitCommandError(self.args, status, errstr)\ngit.exc.GitCommandError: Cmd('git') failed due to: exit code(1)\n  cmdline: git fetch --progress -v rem refs/heads/master\n  stderr: 'fatal: 'origin' does not appear to be a git repository\nfatal: Could not read from remote repository.\n\nPlease make sure you have the correct access rights\nand the repository exists.'\n\n\n# python -c from datalad.support.gitrepo import GitRepo; GitRepo('b').repo.git.fetch()\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"\u003cstring\u003e\", line 1, in \u003cmodule\u003e\n  File \"/home/kyle/src/python/gitpython/git/cmd.py\", line 551, in \u003clambda\u003e\n    return lambda *args, **kwargs: self._call_process(name, *args, **kwargs)\n  File \"/home/kyle/src/python/gitpython/git/cmd.py\", line 1020, in _call_process\n    return self.execute(call, **exec_kwargs)\n  File \"/home/kyle/src/python/gitpython/git/cmd.py\", line 831, in execute\n    raise GitCommandError(command, status, stderr_value, stdout_value)\ngit.exc.GitCommandError: Cmd('git') failed due to: exit code(1)\n  cmdline: git fetch\n  stderr: 'From /tmp/dl-HQ2rgKp/a\n   c229c39..d51c642  master     -\u003e rem/master\nFetching submodule sub\nfatal: 'origin' does not appear to be a git repository\nfatal: Could not read from remote repository.\n\nPlease make sure you have the correct access rights\nand the repository exists.'\n\n\n```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1579542991,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU3NjM3ODYwMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/4038#issuecomment-576378600"},"message":"I've looked into this and pinpointed where it's being dropped.  I'll put together a PR later today.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1579544018,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU3NjM4MzY3NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/4038#issuecomment-576383675"},"message":"Wonderful!","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1579553998,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI5NjQxNzU2NTM="},"added":["fix-implemented"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1579557753,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU3NjQ0MzA1OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/4038#issuecomment-576443059"},"message":"I said:\n\n\u003e I began looking into `GitRepo.fetch` dropping some information. I wonder if this will end up being the same source.\n\nSo, this is kind of the same source, but the fix would be more involved.  GitPython's `RemoteProgress` has a helper (`_parse_progress_line`) that looks at the stderr and decides what to do with it.  Its logic boils down to\n\n  * if it looks like an error line (starts with \"fatal:\" or \"error:\"), store it in `.error_lines` and process all remaining lines as error lines\n  * if it looks like a progress line from git, do the progress thing\n  * otherwise, store it in `.other_lines`\n\n`.other lines` is where \"Fetching submodule sub\" ends up.\n\nI think this approach is pretty unfortunate.  There is so much important information that could be lost.  In the fetch case I show, the \"Fetch submodule\" line is crucial to understanding the error.  And even if the caller looks at `.other_lines`, they don't know where that line came relative to the error line.\n\nMy impression is that resolving it would involve either substantial changes on GitPython's end or large amounts of re-implementation and/or kludges on our end.  Given that our eventual goal is to remove the use of GitPython, our efforts are probably better spent trying to do that, a large part of which is figuring out how to handle progress reporting in clone/fetch/push.  (In other words, it seems silly to spend time to build on top of GitPython if we're just going to turn around an spend time figuring out how to do it another way.)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1579586333,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU3NjUzMDUyNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/4038#issuecomment-576530526"},"message":"@kyleam I agree with your assessment. Given that I have no practical experience with the problem, I am wondering though, what approach would work for us? We need to capture `stderr` to report on errors in a structured way. But if filtering `stderr` to separate progress info from error messages, can't we just let `stderr` send back to `stderr` in \"CC\", i.e. expose `git-clone`s native talk?","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1579586333,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDM0MDk1NDg2Mw=="},"target":"61f10bbfeeeb368e919f2244e62695f2daee523be87c4ab42d1c8574fa48d3fe","message":"@kyleam I agree with your assessment. Given that I have no practical experience with the problem, I am wondering though, what approach would work for us? We need to capture `stderr` to report on errors in a structured way. But if filtering `stderr` to separate progress info from error messages is hard, can't we just let `stderr` send back to `stderr` in \"CC\", i.e. expose `git-clone`s native talk?","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1579586351,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDM0MDk1NDk1Mg=="},"target":"61f10bbfeeeb368e919f2244e62695f2daee523be87c4ab42d1c8574fa48d3fe","message":"@kyleam I agree with your assessment. Given that I have no practical experience with the problem, I am wondering though, what approach would work for us? We need to capture `stderr` to report on errors in a structured way. But if filtering `stderr` to separate progress info from error messages is hard, can't we just let `stderr` go back to `stderr` in \"CC\", i.e. expose `git-clone`s native talk?","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1579586529,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50Mjk2NDk0MjQyOQ=="},"status":2}]}